  <article>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>R7: the closing argument for non-photovoltaic solar</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Sweep executed 18 August 2026. Verdict: <b>closed</b>. On measured,
        system-level numbers, no non-photovoltaic solar technology beats
        photovoltaics on specific power, cost, or readiness. Only unbuilt
        study projections compete, and they require exactly the components
        nobody is building for space. The question is retired from future
        trade studies; this page is the answer they should cite.
      </p>

      <h2>The question</h2>
      <p>
        Does anything that turns sunlight into electricity without a p-n
        junction — heat engines, thermionics, thermophotovoltaics — deserve a
        line in space-power trade studies? The space-unique variants were
        already closed on the <a href="/#closed">kill table</a>. What remained
        was the general case, with current numbers, written once.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <table class="nums">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Technology</th><th>Best measured</th><th>System specific power</th><th>Status</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Solar-dynamic Stirling/Brayton</td>
            <td>29% cycle, <b>17% orbital end-to-end</b> (2 kWe ground test, 800 h, 372 simulated LEO orbits)</td>
            <td><b>3.4 W/kg</b> engineered (Freedom 25 kWe module, 7,440 kg); 10–13 W/kg study-level; 120–700 W/kg projected, never built</td>
            <td>No flight program since 1991; never flown</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Solar thermionics</td>
            <td>6–13% converters (best ~16% at 2,200 K); JPL solar units 7–11% at ~1,900 K</td>
            <td><b>~14 W/kg</b> — one study, 1978; concentrator alone 57% of mass</td>
            <td>Solar program discontinued in the 1970s as uncompetitive with PV</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Solar TPV</td>
            <td>Cell physics: <b>41.1%</b> at 2,400 °C (2022), 44% at 1,435 °C (2024). Complete systems: <b>0.8–3.2%</b> solar-to-electric, measured</td>
            <td>No orbital system estimate exists. Space TPV heritage is nuclear-heated: 100 We class, 6–8 W/kg realistic</td>
            <td>Cells advancing fast; systems stagnant; commercial work is terrestrial storage</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><b>PV baseline</b></td>
            <td>30.7–34% AM0 production III-V cells</td>
            <td><b>50 W/kg</b> rigid; <b>100–200 W/kg</b> flexible flown (UltraFlex 175 W/kg); thin-film cells &gt;1 kW/kg at cell level, TRL 3–4</td>
            <td>Shipping, improving, and getting cheaper (Si entrants target $20/W)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Solar-dynamic: the engine works, the system doesn't</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="class">established</span> The conversion is real: cycle
        efficiencies of 25–35% are documented across Stirling, Brayton, and
        Rankine studies, and NASA's 2 kWe closed-Brayton ground demonstration
        measured 29% cycle efficiency and 17% orbital end-to-end efficiency
        over 800 hours and 372 simulated LEO orbits. The 1968–78 Brayton
        Rotating Unit ran over 38,000 hours without degradation. Nobody is
        disputing the physics.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mass is the kill. The only fully engineered system point —
        Space Station Freedom's 25 kWe module — closed at 7,440 kg:
        <b>3.4 W/kg</b>, with a two-axis concentrator held to ±0.1°, thermal
        storage alone half the system mass, and a radiator rejecting ~4 W for
        every watt delivered. Studies without full engineering reach 10–13
        W/kg with storage, 20–26 W/kg day-only, ~40 W/kg with assumed
        component improvements; Mason's 120–700 W/kg SSP projections require
        inflatable concentrators at 0.2 kg/m² and 2,000 K ceramic turbines —
        components that do not exist. Meanwhile the PV side of that 1991
        trade shipped, and has since improved array-level specific power by
        roughly an order of magnitude.
      </p>

      <h2>Thermionics: abandoned once, correctly</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="class">established</span> JPL's solar-thermionic
        converters ran 7–11% at ~1,900 K for up to 11,000 hours — and the
        program was discontinued in the 1970s, explicitly because it was
        uncompetitive with emerging photovoltaics. Nothing since has changed
        the physics: space charge still halves efficiency, cesium
        neutralization still costs 30–50% of it, emitters still want
        &gt;1,500 K, and the best modern vacuum-gap devices sit at ~10% and
        ~1 W/cm² against a theoretical 30%+/100 W/cm² that no prototype has
        approached. The only system-level study ever done — NASA, 1978, 50
        kWe — closed at ~14 W/kg with the concentrator at 57% of the mass
        budget. That was against 1978 photovoltaics.
      </p>

      <h2>Solar TPV: the live one, and why it still loses</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="class">evidence-backed</span> TPV cell physics is
        genuinely moving: 41.1 ± 1% at a 2,400 °C emitter (Nature, 2022),
        44% at 1,435 °C (Joule, 2024). This is the strongest non-PV thread,
        and it still closes, because a cell is not a system. The two measured
        complete solar-TPV systems stand at 0.8% and 3.2% solar-to-electric —
        against 30%+ direct PV at the same sunlight. Every space application
        of TPV to date is nuclear-heated (radioisotope or reactor), 100
        We-class, with realistic specific power of 6–8 W/kg, limited by the
        cold-side radiator that the low cell rejection temperature forces.
        No orbital solar-TPV system-level W/kg estimate exists in the
        literature; building one from concentrator, emitter, and radiator
        mass models points the wrong way on every term. The active commercial
        TPV work (thermal-battery storage) is terrestrial and does not move
        the space case.
      </p>

      <h2>The incumbent baseline</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="class">evidence-backed</span> Production III-V cells run
        30.7–34% AM0 (Spectrolab XTJ Prime, Rocket Lab/SolAero IMM-α in
        volume at 32%, IMM-β averaging 33.3% BOL). Arrays ship at ~50 W/kg
        rigid and 100–200 W/kg flexible — UltraFlex flew at 175 W/kg. Cost:
        cells run hundreds of $/W ($400–500 per 4×8 cm cell, NASA Spinoff
        2016; Airbus Ventures 2022 concurs); the last rigorous public
        array-level dataset (GSFC 2002) puts delivered arrays at ~$1,650/W.
        Silicon entrants (Solestial, mPower) are attacking exactly that cost
        with a $20/W target, and thin-film/perovskite cells exceed 1 kW/kg at
        cell level, TRL 3–4 — which is what this program's R1 and R2 are
        for. PV is a moving target; non-PV stood still for three decades and
        never caught the 1991 version.
      </p>

      <h2>Verdict</h2>
      <p class="verdict">
        <b>Closed.</b> No non-photovoltaic solar approach beats PV on any
        measured system-level axis; the only competitive figures are unbuilt
        projections depending on components no one is developing for space.
        Future trade studies should cite this page instead of re-opening the
        question.
      </p>

      <h2>What would change the conclusion</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Flight-proven ultra-light concentrators (&lt;0.5 kg/m²), developed for any reason — solar sailing, thermal propulsion. Concentrator mass is the term that killed every thermal scheme.</li>
        <li>A 2,400 °C-class emitter plus spectral-control stack with multi-year space durability — moves solar TPV systems toward their cell physics.</li>
        <li>Mission profiles where PV+battery mass explodes (deep space, eclipse-heavy orbits) — re-run the trade with storage charged to the PV side.</li>
        <li>Anyone flies a non-PV solar demonstrator above 50 W/kg system-level.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Annual check against these four triggers. Otherwise the direction
        stays closed.
      </p>

      <h2>Perimeter and provenance</h2>
      <p>
        Sweep of 18 August 2026, four parallel literature sweeps, twenty
        sources. Findings admitted to the Surya epistemic graph as event
        <code>ev-000000000006</code> with assessment
        <code>surya-assessment-r7-closing-2026-08</code>, judgment
        <b>established-with-gap</b>. Known gaps, flagged there: no current
        merchant III-V $/W primary source (the 2002 GSFC dataset is the last
        rigorous public one; a 2026 market-report figure of $307/W exists but
        its methodology is opaque — treated as indicative, not load-bearing);
        no modern system-level W/kg for thermionics or orbital solar TPV.
        Neither gap can reverse the conclusion: both would require
        order-of-magnitude reversals in technologies that have been static or
        abandoned for decades, while PV kept moving. The earlier kill-table
        figure of 12–25 W/kg for solar-dynamic is refined here: 3.4 W/kg
        engineered, 10–13 W/kg study-level.
      </p>

